INFO:agent.loop:\u26a1 Executing tool: 'knowledge_agent' with arguments: {"source_title": "Pandora Papers reporting on King Abdullah II property network", "source_url": "https://www.theguardian.com/news/2021/oct/03/king-of-jordan-hidden-property-empire-worth-more-than-100m-pandora-papers-reveal", "text": "Benchmark-source reporting states that Guardian analysis of Pandora Papers documents identifies Jordanian national client Abdullah Al Hussein, born 30 January 1962 and associated with Raghadan Palace, as beneficial owner of 16 companies holding assets in the US, UK and Jersey. Named entities include Nabisco Holdings SA, Setara Limited and Timara Limited, which acquired three Malibu clifftop properties. The Guardian reports three Malibu properties: one bought August 2014 for $33.5m and two neighboring properties; three Washington DC condominiums bought in the prior two years for $13.8m total; and seven luxury UK properties bought 2003-2011, including three in Belgravia, with estimated current value about £28m. It reports the offshore firms were incorporated in the British Virgin Islands. Alcogal compliance staff allegedly avoided identifying the client as a politically exposed person and referred to him as 'you know who'; Alcogal denied deliberately failing to identify a PEP and said identity details were recorded in the BVI beneficial ownership secure search system in November 2017. DW reports a different aggregate count: 14 US/UK houses and flats, more than €91m/$106m, through 36 front companies registered in tax havens, with the person referred to as the 'final beneficiary' or 'You know who' from Jordan. Al Jazeera reports three Malibu mansions purchased through three offshore companies for $68m, three Washington apartments, an Ascot house and central London apartments, forming a roughly $100m property empire. The royal palace and DLA Piper, representing Abdullah, stated the properties were personally funded, used for official/private visits, and not funded by state funds, aid, grants or loans; they denied impropriety or misuse of public money. Sources state offshore ownership is not inherently illegal and do not establish wrongdoing. Reported jurisdictions in the broader Pandora Papers include BVI, Panama, Belize, Cyprus, UAE, Singapore and Switzerland; the Abdullah-specific reporting identifies BVI companies and assets in the US, UK and Jersey."}
